data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e4738336-15c2-4978-b461-0b2dc7eeb6f4","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795545305,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Checking the rules, the initial checks for null and empty log are correct. Rule 2 requires handling an empty log with empty collections, focusing on deterministic order. Now considering immutability: tests require comparing key-value pairs and list elements by order. `LinkedHashMap` preserves order. Rule 3 covers logs of length 1, 2, or 3, comparing to the magic bytes. Rule 4 addresses logs of length 4 or greater, again, validating magic bytes. This establishes the initial conditions before phase 1.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e4738336-15c2-4978-b461-0b2dc7eeb6f4","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795545305,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Iterating through the log, starting from an initial position, a series of length checks and type validations are performed. The code verifies record types, payload sizes against a maximum limit, and the structural integrity of the log data. Checksums are calculated and compared to the stored checksums for data integrity, handling potential `UNKNOWN_TYPE` or `RECORD_TOO_LARGE` exceptions. If a checksum mismatch occurs, the valid length is adjusted to reflect the corruption point.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e4738336-15c2-4978-b461-0b2dc7eeb6f4","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795545305,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Checksum validation involves detecting mismatches based on position and record type, throwing exceptions when discrepancies are identified, and adjusting the valid length accordingly. The Fletcher-16 checksum algorithm was verified with an example, confirming the expected output.\n\nPhase 2 focuses on processing intact records and verifying payload layouts based on their types. It outlines precise conditions for record types such as BEGIN, COMMIT, PUT, DELETE, SAVEPOINT, ROLLBACK, CHECKPOINT, and READ, specifying the expected length (`N`) and key/value sizes within each record. The logic checks for violations of length constraints and potential payload data corruption based on the record type.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

